$DIR/main.p:14:3: p was moved to q at line 12, so it can no longer be used here
    owned[W] is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when
    they are dropped. Assigning an owner moves it instead of copying it, so that
    what it owns has exactly one owner. Since line 12, q owns it and p holds
    nothing.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    If q only needs to look at the value, give it a pointer instead:
        q := &p
    If this is the last use of p, move it before line 12, where p still holds
    its value.
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3301" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:14:9: cannot keep the closure mk(i) returns past this iteration of the loop: it is stored in p, declared outside the loop (main.p:11)
    A function that returns a closure builds it in a region its caller provides
    (spec section 11.5). The region of a call in a loop is part of this frame,
    and the call uses the same region on every iteration, so the closure mk(i)
    returns on one iteration is overwritten on the next. Keeping it past the
    iteration, in p, declared outside the loop (main.p:11), would leave it
    changing under you.
    Use the closure within the iteration, or declare the variable that holds it
    inside the loop.
    Go allocates each closure on the heap. Plutonium builds it in the caller's
    frame, which has room for one closure per call site.
    (see "Closures and their regions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3101" for a longer explanation)
